s_list, skills_list, workspace:<UUID>, skill_retrieve, 21n/work-linear-issue, 21n/post-update-on-slack, explicit master approval\n3557:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/archived_sessions/rollout-2026-08-06T09-55-59-019fd552-27bf-7270-8f25-83e477428621.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-10T07:39:14+00:00, thread_id=019fd552-27bf-7270-8f25-83e477428621, closed-world CIMD validation root cause and standards/interoperability distinction)\n3563:## Task 11: Attempt to capture the OAuth metadata defect in the Skillplane Linear backlog, uncertain\n3567:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/06/rollout-2026-08-06T09-55-59-019fd552-27bf-7270-8f25-83e477428621.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-10T07:39:14+00:00, thread_id=019fd552-27bf-7270-8f25-83e477428621, hosted workflow was retrieved but no Linear creation/read-back receipt exists)\n3577:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/05/rollout-2026-08-05T16-14-10-019fd186-0ad0-7d13-a80f-5cb9227dfa13.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-05T10:46:11+00:00, thread_id=019fd186-0ad0-7d13-a80f-5cb9227dfa13, read-only Slack triage found no reply-needed activity)\n3581:- Slack, unread-activity, draft-replies, slack_list_user_conversations, slack_search_public_and_private, #skillplane, limit 100\n3587:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/archived_sessions/rollout-2026-08-03T22-50-42-019fc8a4-5ca5-7af2-8381-e39bd59fc4d0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-04T13:57:51+00:00, thread_id=019fc8a4-5ca5-7af2-8381-e39bd59fc4d0, OAuth root cause narrowed but unproven; SKI-3 backlog entry verified)\n3593:## Task 5: Extract and redesign the Skillplane landing, then deploy it, success\n3597:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/04/rollout-2026-08-04T11-48-40-019fcb6c-9b60-7cc1-820b-e64ffdc0f720.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-05T10:21:37+00:00, thread_id=019fcb6c-9b60-7cc1-820b-e64ffdc0f720, landing extracted to 21n and app/MCP/landing branding deployed)\n3601:- Skillplane, landing/skillplane, @21n/fonts, Twenty One Native, Backgrounds Supply, BrandMark.svelte, skillplane.dev/*, Cloudflare, pnpm@9.12.0, f54057d\n3607:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/archived_sessions/rollout-2026-08-05T17-10-19-019fd1b9-71a0-72b3-a5d7-c5222cf92d29.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-08T12:44:35+00:00, thread_id=019fd1b9-71a0-72b3-a5d7-c5222cf92d29, SKI-1 published to draft PR #3; hosted amendment candidate remains review-required)\n3615:- when the user required “use cached skill if skillplane MCP is not available” and “use local latest mcpfn worktree” -> resolve the hosted workflow first, then use the newest local dependency worktree rather than published packages when requested. [Task 12]\n3616:- when the user said “No scaffolding or shims or backward support needed in skillplane” -> remove legacy paths rather than adding compatibility wrappers or dual production writers. [Task 12]\n3618:- when ideating Skillplane local/cloud workspaces, analytics, MCP mutations, or ephemeral agents -> address those dimensions directly; the user is exploring design, not authorizing implementation. [Task 13]\n3619:- when the user writes `splane <slug-or-skill-name>` -> use Skillplane MCP to discover, retrieve, and follow the hosted skill; resolve the authorized workspace and exact published version live, never substitute the local repository or reconstruct instructions from memory. [ad-hoc note]\n3626:- when the user asked to move the landing into `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/21n/landing/skillplane` -> isolate the requested landing work there and preserve unrelated destination-checkout changes. [Task 5]\n3629:- when the user asks to “improve skillplane web app onboarding to suit real production use” -> deliver a complete auth-to-app flow, not a runtime diagnostic as the user-facing homepage. [Task 7]\n3632:- when the user wrote `splane add to backlog` -> retrieve the exact published Skillplane workflow through live MCP before any Linear mutation; backlog capture alone does not authorize code changes, commits, deployment, assignments, or dates. [Task 11]\n3636:- SKI-6 moved Skillplane to a single McpFn-owned runtime and hosted authorization path without shims or fallback writers; McpFn owns protocol/runtime and MCP-specific authorization compatibility, while the authority owns identity/session/consent/token/security state and Skillplane keeps tenancy, grants, approval, domain policy, and tool behavior. The exact source pin is `42a0f2c3876adb47fb26dfc187f6fea41f436719` with `sha256:445472059355d481bbf34022dceea6b86ae4cc452562ca628109a8c7e9e509f3`. [Task 12]\n3637:- For SKI-6, `pnpm mcpfn:verify`, `pnpm test:mcp:contract`, and `pnpm test:mcp:conformance` are the actual Skillplane commands; local/remote McpFn release gates, Auth 57/57, Inspector 6/6, Skillplane contract 8/8, and authenticated conformance 1/1 passed. Draft PR #7 still uses local `link:` dependencies, and exact published-package replacement, controlled Claude/ChatGPT flows, deployment proof, and the late marker edge case remain pending. [Task 12]\n3641:- Production topology is `skillplane.dev` landing zone route, `app.skillplane.dev` Custom Domain, and `mcp.skillplane.dev/mcp` Custom Domain. Workers access Railway Postgres through Cloudflare Hyperdrive; R2 bundles remain private; the app owns Email Service and Turnstile. [Task 1]\n"},"started_at_ms":1788013254828,"completed_at_ms":1788013254828}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T14:20:54.843Z","ordinal":15912,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a041ae-ce65-7631-a9e6-9a4d22e9067d","turn_id":"01a04de4-b2d2-7b13-8755-cb4387b230b8","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-9f8586b9-8df9-4cb0-a713-5dcdb2d775e9","process_id":"67409","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,420p' /Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,420p' /Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"---\nname: pr-refetch-fix\ndescription: Refetch the latest GitHub PR review feedback reliably before applying fixes. Use when a user asks to re-check review comments after a push, says there are newer PR comments that were missed, or when timestamp-only PR comment queries may be incomplete or misleading. This skill is for GitHub PR review triage where unresolved review threads, inline comments, review bodies, and issue comments must be checked together.\n---\n\n# PR Refetch Fix\n\nRefetch PR feedback from the right GitHub surfaces before deciding there are no new issues. Do not rely only on a timestamp cutoff against inline review comments.\n\n## Core Rule\n\nWhen the user asks for \"newer PR comments\", \"latest review issues\", or says comments were missed:\n\n1. Check current unresolved review threads first.\n2. Treat timestamp filtering as secondary, not primary.\n3. Verify every still-open thread against the current worktree before fixing or dismissing it.\n\nThe failure mode this skill prevents is:\n\n- querying only `pulls/{n}/comments` after an incorrect cutoff time\n- missing comments posted in review threads or review bodies\n- missing still-unresolved older threads that remain actionable after newer pushes\n\n## Workflow\n\n### 1. Identify the PRs and auth context\n\nUse the user's existing GitHub auth context if they specified one earlier, such as:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\n```\n\nPreserve that context for every `gh` call in the workflow.\n\n### 1a. Work only in the PR's corresponding worktree and branch\n\nBefore checking comments or making fixes:\n\n1. switch to the PR's dedicated worktree\n2. confirm the checked-out branch is the PR head branch\n3. inspect and edit only in that worktree\n\nDo not:\n\n- inspect comments against the main repo checkout if the PR has a separate worktree\n- validate a review comment against `next`, `dev`, or another sibling PR branch\n- make fixes in one worktree and assume they apply to another PR branch\n\nIf multiple PRs are involved, handle each PR in its own worktree independently.\n\nMinimum check:\n\n```bash\ngit branch --show-current\ngit status --short\ngit remote -v\n```\n\nUse the worktree that already backs the PR whenever available. Treat that worktree as the source of truth for:\n\n- whether a comment is already fixed\n- whether a thread is stale\n- what code should be edited\n\n### 2. Do not start with timestamp-only filtering\n\nDo not begin with:\n\n- only `gh pr view --json comments`\n- only `gh api repos/.../pulls/<n>/comments`\n- only \"comments after last pushed commit time\"\n\nThose can miss actionable review feedback.\n\n### 3. Fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n\nThis is the primary source of truth for \"what still needs attention\".\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql -f query='\nquery {\n  repository(owner:\"OWNER\", name:\"REPO\") {\n    pullRequest(number:PR_NUMBER) {\n      reviewThreads(first:100) {\n        nodes {\n          isResolved\n          path\n          comments(first:20) {\n            nodes {\n              body\n              author { login }\n              createdAt\n              outdated\n              url\n              diffHunk\n              originalLine\n              line\n            }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}'\n```\n\nIf there may be more than 100 threads, page through the connection instead of assuming the first page is complete.\n\n### 4. Also check the other review surfaces\n\nAfter review threads, inspect:\n\n1. PR reviews\n2. PR issue comments\n3. Inline review comments if needed for raw export or extra metadata\n\nUseful commands:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\n```\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\n```\n\nUse these to supplement threads, not replace them.\n\n### 5. Use timestamps carefully\n\nIf the user explicitly wants \"since the last push\", compute the cutoff from the exact pushed commit, not a guessed time.\n\nRules:\n\n- use the exact commit SHA that was pushed\n- use its absolute timestamp in UTC\n- if the user seems confused about \"today\", \"after that push\", or \"latest\", mention exact dates/times\n\nEven with an accurate cutoff, do not exclude unresolved review threads just because the thread started earlier. A still-unresolved thread can remain actionable after later pushes.\n\n### 6. Classify findings before editing\n\nFor each thread or comment:\n\n`Actionable`\n- thread is unresolved, and\n- the comment still matches the current file state, and\n- the issue is not already fixed in the worktree\n\n`Already fixed / stale`\n- thread is outdated, or\n- the code already addresses it, or\n- the comment refers to an earlier diff shape that no longer exists\n\n`Non-code noise`\n- Graphite merge queue comments\n- usage-limit notices\n- bot bookkeeping without code findings\n\n### 7. Verify against the current worktree\n\nBefore editing:\n\n1. open the current file in the worktree\n2. inspect the exact code around the commented path/line\n3. decide whether the review comment is still valid now\n\nDo not trust the review diff alone. Many bot comments are attached to old hunks and become stale after subsequent pushes.\n\n### 8. Fix only the valid items\n\nWhen fixing:\n\n- prefer narrow fixes tied to the still-valid review findings\n- add focused regression coverage when practical\n- keep unrelated local or `.conduct/` artifacts out of commits unless the user explicitly asks for them\n\n### 9. Report with explicit buckets\n\nSummarize findings as:\n\n- `Fixed`\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed)`\n- `Not Attempted`\n\nIf something remains pending because of extracted-worktree dependency issues, say so explicitly.\n\n## Decision Heuristics\n\nUse unresolved threads as the main queue when the user says:\n\n- \"check newer PR comments\"\n- \"there are still comments\"\n- \"you missed some issues\"\n- \"refetch review feedback\"\n\nUse timestamp filtering only to answer narrower questions like:\n\n- \"what comments arrived after commit <sha>?\"\n- \"what was posted after the last push?\"\n\nEven then, compare the result against current unresolved threads before concluding there is nothing new.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n- Missing comments because `gh pr view --json comments` does not include review threads.\n- Missing comments because `pulls/<n>/comments` was queried with the wrong cutoff time.\n- Treating all old comments as irrelevant even when the thread is still unresolved.\n- Treating all unresolved bot comments as actionable without checking the current code.\n- Forgetting that some PR activity is non-code noise, especially merge-queue comments.\n\n## Minimum Successful Pass\n\nA correct refetch pass should:\n\n1. fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n2. inspect review bodies and issue comments\n3. cross-check against the current worktree\n4. separate valid issues from stale ones\n5. only then start fixing code\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: pr-refetch-fix\ndescription: Refetch the latest GitHub PR review feedback reliably before applying fixes. Use when a user asks to re-check review comments after a push, says there are newer PR comments that were missed, or when timestamp-only PR comment queries may be incomplete or misleading. This skill is for GitHub PR review triage where unresolved review threads, inline comments, review bodies, and issue comments must be checked together.\n---\n\n# PR Refetch Fix\n\nRefetch PR feedback from the right GitHub surfaces before deciding there are no new issues. Do not rely only on a timestamp cutoff against inline review comments.\n\n## Core Rule\n\nWhen the user asks for \"newer PR comments\", \"latest review issues\", or says comments were missed:\n\n1. Check current unresolved review threads first.\n2. Treat timestamp filtering as secondary, not primary.\n3. Verify every still-open thread against the current worktree before fixing or dismissing it.\n\nThe failure mode this skill prevents is:\n\n- querying only `pulls/{n}/comments` after an incorrect cutoff time\n- missing comments posted in review threads or review bodies\n- missing still-unresolved older threads that remain actionable after newer pushes\n\n## Workflow\n\n### 1. Identify the PRs and auth context\n\nUse the user's existing GitHub auth context if they specified one earlier, such as:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\n```\n\nPreserve that context for every `gh` call in the workflow.\n\n### 1a. Work only in the PR's corresponding worktree and branch\n\nBefore checking comments or making fixes:\n\n1. switch to the PR's dedicated worktree\n2. confirm the checked-out branch is the PR head branch\n3. inspect and edit only in that worktree\n\nDo not:\n\n- inspect comments against the main repo checkout if the PR has a separate worktree\n- validate a review comment against `next`, `dev`, or another sibling PR branch\n- make fixes in one worktree and assume they apply to another PR branch\n\nIf multiple PRs are involved, handle each PR in its own worktree independently.\n\nMinimum check:\n\n```bash\ngit branch --show-current\ngit status --short\ngit remote -v\n```\n\nUse the worktree that already backs the PR whenever available. Treat that worktree as the source of truth for:\n\n- whether a comment is already fixed\n- whether a thread is stale\n- what code should be edited\n\n### 2. Do not start with timestamp-only filtering\n\nDo not begin with:\n\n- only `gh pr view --json comments`\n- only `gh api repos/.../pulls/<n>/comments`\n- only \"comments after last pushed commit time\"\n\nThose can miss actionable review feedback.\n\n### 3. Fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n\nThis is the primary source of truth for \"what still needs attention\".\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql -f query='\nquery {\n  repository(owner:\"OWNER\", name:\"REPO\") {\n    pullRequest(number:PR_NUMBER) {\n      reviewThreads(first:100) {\n        nodes {\n          isResolved\n          path\n          comments(first:20) {\n            nodes {\n              body\n              author { login }\n              createdAt\n              outdated\n              url\n              diffHunk\n              originalLine\n              line\n            }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}'\n```\n\nIf there may be more than 100 threads, page through the connection instead of assuming the first page is complete.\n\n### 4. Also check the other review surfaces\n\nAfter review threads, inspect:\n\n1. PR reviews\n2. PR issue comments\n3. Inline review comments if needed for raw export or extra metadata\n\nUseful commands:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\n```\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\n```\n\nUse these to supplement threads, not replace them.\n\n### 5. Use timestamps carefully\n\nIf the user explicitly wants \"since the last push\", compute the cutoff from the exact pushed commit, not a guessed time.\n\nRules:\n\n- use the exact commit SHA that was pushed\n- use its absolute timestamp in UTC\n- if the user seems confused about \"today\", \"after that push\", or \"latest\", mention exact dates/times\n\nEven with an accurate cutoff, do not exclude unresolved review threads just because the thread started earlier. A still-unresolved thread can remain actionable after later pushes.\n\n### 6. Classify findings before editing\n\nFor each thread or comment:\n\n`Actionable`\n- thread is unresolved, and\n- the comment still matches the current file state, and\n- the issue is not already fixed in the worktree\n\n`Already fixed / stale`\n- thread is outdated, or\n- the code already addresses it, or\n- the comment refers to an earlier diff shape that no longer exists\n\n`Non-code noise`\n- Graphite merge queue comments\n- usage-limit notices\n- bot bookkeeping without code findings\n\n### 7. Verify against the current worktree\n\nBefore editing:\n\n1. open the current file in the worktree\n2. inspect the exact code around the commented path/line\n3. decide whether the review comment is still valid now\n\nDo not trust the review diff alone. Many bot comments are attached to old hunks and become stale after subsequent pushes.\n\n### 8. Fix only the valid items\n\nWhen fixing:\n\n- prefer narrow fixes tied to the still-valid review findings\n- add focused regression coverage when practical\n- keep unrelated local or `.conduct/` artifacts out of commits unless the user explicitly asks for them\n\n### 9. Report with explicit buckets\n\nSummarize findings as:\n\n- `Fixed`\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed)`\n- `Not Attempted`\n\nIf something remains pending because of extracted-worktree dependency issues, say so explicitly.\n\n## Decision Heuristics\n\nUse unresolved threads as the main queue when the user says:\n\n- \"check newer PR comments\"\n- \"there are still comments\"\n- \"you missed some issues\"\n- \"refetch review feedback\"\n\nUse timestamp filtering only to answer narrower questions like:\n\n- \"what comments arrived after commit <sha>?\"\n- \"what was posted after the last push?\"\n\nEven then, compare the result against current unresolved threads before concluding there is nothing new.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n- Missing comments because `gh pr view --json comments` does not include review threads.\n- Missing comments because `pulls/<n>/comments` was queried with the wrong cutoff time.\n- Treating all old comments as irrelevant even when the thread is still unresolved.\n- Treating all unresolved bot comments as actionable without checking the current code.\n- Forgetting that some PR activity is non-code noise, especially merge-queue comments.\n\n## Minimum Successful Pass\n\nA correct refetch pass should:\n\n1. fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n2. inspect review bodies and issue comments\n3. cross-check against the current worktree\n4. separate valid issues from stale ones\n5. only then start fixing code\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1625},"formatted_output":"---\nname: pr-refetch-fix\ndescription: Refetch the latest GitHub PR review feedback reliably before applying fixes. Use when a user asks to re-check review comments after a push, says there are newer PR comments that were missed, or when timestamp-only PR comment queries may be incomplete or misleading. This skill is for GitHub PR review triage where unresolved review threads, inline comments, review bodies, and issue comments must be checked together.\n---\n\n# PR Refetch Fix\n\nRefetch PR feedback from the right GitHub surfaces before deciding there are no new issues. Do not rely only on a timestamp cutoff against inline review comments.\n\n## Core Rule\n\nWhen the user asks for \"newer PR comments\", \"latest review issues\", or says comments were missed:\n\n1. Check current unresolved review threads first.\n2. Treat timestamp filtering as secondary, not primary.\n3. Verify every still-open thread against the current worktree before fixing or dismissing it.\n\nThe failure mode this skill prevents is:\n\n- querying only `pulls/{n}/comments` after an incorrect cutoff time\n- missing comments posted in review threads or review bodies\n- missing still-unresolved older threads that remain actionable after newer pushes\n\n## Workflow\n\n### 1. Identify the PRs and auth context\n\nUse the user's existing GitHub auth context if they specified one earlier, such as:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\n```\n\nPreserve that context for every `gh` call in the workflow.\n\n### 1a. Work only in the PR's corresponding worktree and branch\n\nBefore checking comments or making fixes:\n\n1. switch to the PR's dedicated worktree\n2. confirm the checked-out branch is the PR head branch\n3. inspect and edit only in that worktree\n\nDo not:\n\n- inspect comments against the main repo checkout if the PR has a separate worktree\n- validate a review comment against `next`, `dev`, or another sibling PR branch\n- make fixes in one worktree and assume they apply to another PR branch\n\nIf multiple PRs are involved, handle each PR in its own worktree independently.\n\nMinimum check:\n\n```bash\ngit branch --show-current\ngit status --short\ngit remote -v\n```\n\nUse the worktree that already backs the PR whenever available. Treat that worktree as the source of truth for:\n\n- whether a comment is already fixed\n- whether a thread is stale\n- what code should be edited\n\n### 2. Do not start with timestamp-only filtering\n\nDo not begin with:\n\n- only `gh pr view --json comments`\n- only `gh api repos/.../pulls/<n>/comments`\n- only \"comments after last pushed commit time\"\n\nThose can miss actionable review feedback.\n\n### 3. Fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n\nThis is the primary source of truth for \"what still needs attention\".\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql -f query='\nquery {\n  repository(owner:\"OWNER\", name:\"REPO\") {\n    pullRequest(number:PR_NUMBER) {\n      reviewThreads(first:100) {\n        nodes {\n          isResolved\n          path\n          comments(first:20) {\n            nodes {\n              body\n              author { login }\n              createdAt\n              outdated\n              url\n              diffHunk\n              originalLine\n              line\n            }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}'\n```\n\nIf there may be more than 100 threads, page through the connection instead of assuming the first page is complete.\n\n### 4. Also check the other review surfaces\n\nAfter review threads, inspect:\n\n1. PR reviews\n2. PR issue comments\n3. Inline review comments if needed for raw export or extra metadata\n\nUseful commands:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\n```\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\n```\n\nUse these to supplement threads, not replace them.\n\n### 5. Use timestamps carefully\n\nIf the user explicitly wants \"since the last push\", compute the cutoff from the exact pushed commit, not a guessed time.\n\nRules:\n\n- use the exact commit SHA that was pushed\n- use its absolute timestamp in UTC\n- if the user seems confused about \"today\", \"after that push\", or \"latest\", mention exact dates/times\n\nEven with an accurate cutoff, do not exclude unresolved review threads just because the thread started earlier. A still-unresolved thread can remain actionable after later pushes.\n\n### 6. Classify findings before editing\n\nFor each thread or comment:\n\n`Actionable`\n- thread is unresolved, and\n- the comment still matches the current file state, and\n- the issue is not already fixed in the worktree\n\n`Already fixed / stale`\n- thread is outdated, or\n- the code already addresses it, or\n- the comment refers to an earlier diff shape that no longer exists\n\n`Non-code noise`\n- Graphite merge queue comments\n- usage-limit notices\n- bot bookkeeping without code findings\n\n### 7. Verify against the current worktree\n\nBefore editing:\n\n1. open the current file in the worktree\n2. inspect the exact code around the commented path/line\n3. decide whether the review comment is still valid now\n\nDo not trust the review diff alone. Many bot comments are attached to old hunks and become stale after subsequent pushes.\n\n### 8. Fix only the valid items\n\nWhen fixing:\n\n- prefer narrow fixes tied to the still-valid review findings\n- add focused regression coverage when practical\n- keep unrelated local or `.conduct/` artifacts out of commits unless the user explicitly asks for them\n\n### 9. Report with explicit buckets\n\nSummarize findings as:\n\n- `Fixed`\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed)`\n- `Not Attempted`\n\nIf something remains pending because of extracted-worktree dependency issues, say so explicitly.\n\n## Decision Heuristics\n\nUse unresolved threads as the main queue when the user says:\n\n- \"check newer PR comments\"\n- \"there are still comments\"\n- \"you missed some issues\"\n- \"refetch review feedback\"\n\nUse timestamp filtering only to answer narrower questions like:\n\n- \"what comments arrived after commit <sha>?\"\n- \"what was posted after the last push?\"\n\nEven then, compare the result against current unresolved threads before concluding there is nothing new.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n- Missing comments because `gh pr view --json comments` does not include review threads.\n- Missing comments because `pulls/<n>/comments` was queried with the wrong cutoff time.\n- Treating all old comments as irrelevant even when the thread is still unresolved.\n- Treating all unresolved bot comments as actionable without checking the current code.\n- Forgetting that some PR activity is non-code noise, especially merge-queue comments.\n\n## Minimum Successful Pass\n\nA correct refetch pass should:\n\n1. fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n2. inspect review bodies and issue comments\n3. cross-check against the current worktree\n4. separate valid issues from stale ones\n5. only then start fixing code\n"},"started_at_ms":1788013254842,"completed_at_ms":1788013254843}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T14:20:54.846Z","ordinal":15913,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a04de5-18be-75b0-b7bb-d761c30a036d","call_id":"call_EO7xJkwmhdRehPUZ8uZnhFKF","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"57e72f\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000001625,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":1709,\"output\":\"---\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\ndescription: Refetch the latest GitHub PR review feedback reliably before applying fixes. Use when a user asks to re-check review comments after a push, says there are newer PR comments that were missed, or when timestamp-only PR comment queries may be incomplete or misleading. This skill is for GitHub PR review triage where unresolved review threads, inline comments, review bodies, and issue comments must be checked together.\\n---\\n\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\n\\nRefetch PR feedback from the right GitHub surfaces before deciding there are no new issues. Do not rely only on a timestamp cutoff against inline review comments.\\n\\n## Core Rule\\n\\nWhen the user asks for \\\"newer PR comments\\\", \\\"latest review issues\\\", or says comments were missed:\\n\\n1. Check current unresolved review threads first.\\n2. Treat timestamp filtering as secondary, not primary.\\n3. Verify every still-open thread against the current worktree before fixing or dismissing it.\\n\\nThe failure mode this skill prevents is:\\n\\n- querying only `pulls/{n}/comments` after an incorrect cutoff time\\n- missing comments posted in review threads or review bodies\\n- missing still-unresolved older threads that remain actionable after newer pushes\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n### 1. Identify the PRs and auth context\\n\\nUse the user's existing GitHub auth context if they specified one earlier, such as:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\n```\\n\\nPreserve that context for every `gh` call in the workflow.\\n\\n### 1a. Work only in the PR's corresponding worktree and branch\\n\\nBefore checking comments or making fixes:\\n\\n1. switch to the PR's dedicated worktree\\n2. confirm the checked-out branch is the PR head branch\\n3. inspect and edit only in that worktree\\n\\nDo not:\\n\\n- inspect comments against the main repo checkout if the PR has a separate worktree\\n- validate a review comment against `next`, `dev`, or another sibling PR branch\\n- make fixes in one worktree and assume they apply to another PR branch\\n\\nIf multiple PRs are involved, handle each PR in its own worktree independently.\\n\\nMinimum check:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit branch --show-current\\ngit status --short\\ngit remote -v\\n```\\n\\nUse the worktree that already backs the PR whenever available. Treat that worktree as the source of truth for:\\n\\n- whether a comment is already fixed\\n- whether a thread is stale\\n- what code should be edited\\n\\n### 2. Do not start with timestamp-only filtering\\n\\nDo not begin with:\\n\\n- only `gh pr view --json comments`\\n- only `gh api repos/.../pulls/<n>/comments`\\n- only \\\"comments after last pushed commit time\\\"\\n\\nThose can miss actionable review feedback.\\n\\n### 3. Fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\\n\\nThis is the primary source of truth for \\\"what still needs attention\\\".\\n\\nUse:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql -f query='\\nquery {\\n  repository(owner:\\\"OWNER\\\", name:\\\"REPO\\\") {\\n    pullRequest(number:PR_NUMBER) {\\n      reviewThreads(first:100) {\\n        nodes {\\n          isResolved\\n          path\\n          comments(first:20) {\\n            nodes {\\n              body\\n              author { login }\\n              createdAt\\n              outdated\\n              url\\n              diffHunk\\n              originalLine\\n              line\\n            }\\n          }\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}'\\n```\\n\\nIf there may be more than 100 threads, page through the connection instead of assuming the first page is complete.\\n\\n### 4. Also check the other review surfaces\\n\\nAfter review threads, inspect:\\n\\n1. PR reviews\\n2. PR issue comments\\n3. Inline review comments if needed for raw export or extra metadata\\n\\nUseful commands:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\n```\\n\\nUse these to supplement threads, not replace them.\\n\\n### 5. Use timestamps carefully\\n\\nIf the user explicitly wants \\\"since the last push\\\", compute the cutoff from the exact pushed commit, not a guessed time.\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n- use the exact commit SHA that was pushed\\n- use its absolute timestamp in UTC\\n- if the user seems confused about \\\"today\\\", \\\"after that push\\\", or \\\"latest\\\", mention exact dates/times\\n\\nEven with an accurate cutoff, do not exclude unresolved review threads just because the thread started earlier. A still-unresolved thread can remain actionable after later pushes.\\n\\n### 6. Classify findings before editing\\n\\nFor each thread or comment:\\n\\n`Actionable`\\n- thread is unresolved, and\\n- the comment still matches the current file state, and\\n- the issue is not already fixed in the worktree\\n\\n`Already fixed / stale`\\n- thread is outdated, or\\n- the code already addresses it, or\\n- the comment refers to an earlier diff shape that no longer exists\\n\\n`Non-code noise`\\n- Graphite merge queue comments\\n- usage-limit notices\\n- bot bookkeeping without code findings\\n\\n### 7. Verify against the current worktree\\n\\nBefore editing:\\n\\n1. open the current file in the worktree\\n2. inspect the exact code around the commented path/line\\n3. decide whether the review comment is still valid now\\n\\nDo not trust the review diff alone. Many bot comments are attached to old hunks and become stale after subsequent pushes.\\n\\n### 8. Fix only the valid items\\n\\nWhen fixing:\\n\\n- prefer narrow fixes tied to the still-valid review findings\\n- add focused regression coverage when practical\\n- keep unrelated local or `.conduct/` artifacts out of commits unless the user explicitly asks for them\\n\\n### 9. Report with explicit buckets\\n\\nSummarize findings as:\\n\\n- `Fixed`\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed)`\\n- `Not Attempted`\\n\\nIf something remains pending because of extracted-worktree dependency issues, say so explicitly.\\n\\n## Decision Heuristics\\n\\nUse unresolved threads as the main queue when the user says:\\n\\n- \\\"check newer PR comments\\\"\\n- \\\"there are still comments\\\"\\n- \\\"you missed some issues\\\"\\n- \\\"refetch review feedback\\\"\\n\\nUse timestamp filtering only to answer narrower questions like:\\n\\n- \\\"what comments arrived after commit <sha>?\\\"\\n- \\\"what was posted after the last push?\\\"\\n\\nEven then, compare the result against current unresolved threads before concluding there is nothing new.\\n\\n## Common Pitfalls\\n\\n- Missing comments because `gh pr view --json comments` does not include review threads.\\n- Missing comments because `pulls/<n>/comments` was queried with the wrong cutoff time.\\n- Treating all old comments as irrelevant even when the thread is still unresolved.\\n- Treating all unresolved bot comments as actionable without checking the current code.\\n- Forgetting that some PR activity is non-code noise, especially merge-queue comments.\\n\\n## Minimum Successful Pass\\n\\nA correct refetch pass should:\\n\\n1. fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\\n2. inspect review bodies and issue comments\\n3. cross-check against the current worktree\\n4. separate valid issues from stale ones\\n5. only then start fixing code\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"692ee0\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.00000325,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":7282,\"output\":\"170:- MCP-2, McpFn, work-linear-issue@1.0.1, oauth-core 0.0.2, tsup, ESM, CommonJS, gate:mcpfn-release, reviewThreads, force-with-lease, PR-122, UNSTABLE\\n600:# Task Group: August 23 Skillplane PR stacks, bounded review, and multi-region audit\\n601:scope: Use for GitHub-native Skillplane stacked PR status, hosted bounded review-fix rounds, or read-only multi-region architecture analysis.\\n602:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane; reuse_rule=workflow mechanics are reusable; PR heads, checks, mergeability, architecture state, and deployment evidence require live revalidation.\\n604:## Task 1: Review Skillplane PR #3 through bounded hosted rounds, success\\n608:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T12-55-41-01a02d82-ccc1-7683-926d-46dfe638708b.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-23T17:34:36+00:00, thread_id=01a02d82-ccc1-7683-926d-46dfe638708b, two one-push review-fix rounds then a no-op final review)\\n612:- splane pr-review-fix, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #3, SKI-1, d398cba6, reviewThreads, outdated, repeatable-read, applicationCommit\\n618:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T12-48-36-01a02d7c-5037-7be3-a0bc-20dbd494c482.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-23T17:37:23+00:00, thread_id=01a02d7c-5037-7be3-a0bc-20dbd494c482, native stack status was read-only and time-specific)\\n624:## Task 3: Audit Skillplane multi-region architecture read-only, success\\n628:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T22-10-13-01a02f7e-7c4f-7941-9a73-463779dc38ff.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-23T16:52:53+00:00, thread_id=01a02f7e-7c4f-7941-9a73-463779dc38ff, static architecture guidance; no implementation/tests)\\n636:- when the user writes `splane <slug-or-skill-name>` -> resolve the authorized workspace and exact published Skillplane skill live through MCP; do not substitute local repository instructions or reconstruct the skill from memory. [ad-hoc note]\\n643:- `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1` uses unresolved GraphQL threads plus review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments as one snapshot; after an authorized push, verify heads once and do not refetch/poll. An unresolved thread can be historical: inspect `outdated`, current code/commit, and addressed markers. [Task 1]\\n646:- Skillplane’s recommended target is central AuthFn/OAuth/workspace-directory authorization followed by `workspace_id -> region_id/routing_epoch`, a short-lived internal assertion, and private Service Binding forwarding to regional single-writer workspace cells. Reject stale routes with `409 WORKSPACE_REGION_MISMATCH` before side effects. [Task 3]\\n652:- Nucleus’s user-home-region implementation is a pattern library, not a deployable drop-in: Skillplane identities can access multiple workspace regions, and R2 location hints are not Indian-residency guarantees. [Task 3]\\n654:# Task Group: August 23 Skillplane MCPJam OAuth and Diffpanel publication\\n655:scope: Use for local Skillplane MCP OAuth/tunnel diagnosis, scoped skill-creation authorization, or immutable Diffpanel branch reviews.\\n656:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane; reuse_rule=configuration/procedure is reusable; credentials, tunnel/process state, PR metadata, and Diffpanel runs require live inspection.\\n662:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T11-38-16-01a02d3b-e931-7603-ba18-b6cb2a60a242.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-23T07:02:13+00:00, thread_id=01a02d3b-e931-7603-ba18-b6cb2a60a242, live tunnel repair plus authorization diagnosis)\\n672:- rollout_summaries/2026-0